course: dictionary
lesson: 4458
title: Harbor: a safe place, and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A harbor is a sheltered part of the sea where ships and boats can stay safe from big waves and storms. Harbor is the US spelling; in the UK it is spelled harbour. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: «The Soviet Union was attacked by Germany in June 1941, and the USA joined the war after Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941.» Pearl Harbor is a place name, so here it means a harbor where ships are kept. The words attacked and war are clues that this was a dangerous event.
explain: Harbor can also be a verb. To harbor someone means to hide or shelter them. To harbor a feeling means to keep it in your mind for a long time. Ask yourself: does the sentence show a place, a person, or a feeling? That tells you which meaning to use.
fact: A harbor is a sheltered area of water by the coast where ships and boats can stay safely.
fact: «Harbor» is the US spelling; «harbour» is the UK spelling, as in Sydney Harbour.
fact: To harbor someone means to hide or shelter them, often someone wanted by the police.
fact: To harbor a grudge means to keep an angry feeling in your mind for a long time.
fact: To harbor germs or pests means to let them live and grow in a place.
exercise: In «Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941», what does Pearl Harbor mean? | A school for sailors | *A sheltered area of water where ships can stay safe | A kind of loud drum | A tall hill covered in trees
exercise: Sam says, «She has harbored a grudge against him for years.» What does harbored a grudge mean? | She has forgotten all about it | She has given him a present | *She has kept an angry feeling inside for years | She has lost his address
exercise: Mia says harbor and harbour are different words. What is true? | Only harbour means a place for boats | They mean totally different things | Harbour is a verb and harbor is a noun | *They are the same word, spelled in US and UK ways
test: The police found that the thief was harbored by an old friend. What does harbored mean here? | Forgot | Painted | *Hid and sheltered | Argued with
test: The boats rocked gently in the harbor, safe from the storm. Where were the boats? | In a cave under a mountain | *In a sheltered area of water by the coast | On a hill far from the sea | In a field of wheat
exam: Science book says, «Damp walls can harbor mould and germs.» What does harbor mean? | To paint them bright red | *To let them live and grow there | To sell them quickly | To wash them away
